  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "A particle moves from the origin with a constant velocity of $(i-2j)\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Find the distance from the particle to the origin after $5$ hours.",
  "worked_solution": "If a particle moves with constant velocity $u,$  the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=i-2j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=5\\,\\text{h}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & ={r}_{0}+ut \\\\  & =(0i+0j)+(i-2j)(5) \\\\  & =(5i-10j)\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the particle to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|5i-10j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-10)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
